        "id": 0,
        "original_id": "24788f69-bf3d-5344-b10c-86f1a292bde2",
        "text": "This chunk is about:\n- People: Heinrich Zimmer, Max Müller, Paul Tillich, Rajendralal Mitra\n- Roles: German scholar, philologist, Orientalist, Indologist, philosopher\n- Concepts: Indian Philology, Indology, transtheism, theism, atheism\n- Events: Bengal Renaissance\n\nFacts:\n- Max Müller was born on 6 December 1823 and died on 28 October 1900.\n- Max Müller was a German-born philologist and Orientalist who lived and studied in Britain for most of his life.\n- Max Müller wrote both scholarly and popular works on Indology.\n- Rajendralal Mitra was born on 15 February 1824 and died on 26 July 1891.\n- Rajendralal Mitra was the first modern Indologist of Indian origin and a key figure in the Bengal Renaissance.\n- Heinrich Zimmer was regarded as the most important German scholar in Indian Philology after Max Müller.\n- Transtheism is a term coined by either Paul Tillich or Heinrich Zimmer for a religious philosophy that is neither theistic nor atheistic, but beyond both.",
        "type": "TextSummary"
      },
      {
        "id": 1,
        "original_id": "e5718cd3-d6f6-56ba-b5cd-28e3aec6c66a",
        "text": "Page: Heinrich Zimmer. Sentence: He was the most important German scholar in Indian Philology after Max Müller (1823-1900).\nPage: Max Müller. Sentence: Friedrich Max Müller (6 December 1823 – 28 October 1900), generally known as Max Müller, was a German-born philologist and Orientalist, who lived and studied in Britain for most of his life.\nPage: Max Müller. Sentence: Müller wrote both scholarly and popular works on the subject of Indology.\nPage: Transtheism. Sentence: Transtheism is a term coined by either philosopher Paul Tillich or Indologist Heinrich Zimmer referring to a system of thought or religious philosophy which is neither theistic, nor atheistic, but is beyond them.\nPage: Rajendralal Mitra. Sentence: Raja Rajendralal Mitra (15 February 1824 – 26 July 1891) was the first modern Indologist of Indian origin, and was a key figure in the Bengal Renaissance.",
